Snort can be installed on computer host. In terms of CPU, memory consumption and the packet drop rate via the network interface card, the performance is directly proportional to the computer host performance.

Snort is facing major problems with the continuing rise in network speed and malicious traffic. In order to detect malicious traffic, it must process greater traffic volumes and inspect each incoming network packet. It must process a large amount of network traffic that can achieve speeds of up to 10 Gbps in order for Snort to have superior efficiency. If the packet inspection fails to run at the appropriate pace, the packets will be lost and allow undetected malicious packets to reach the computer network.Hence, Snort should also be sufficiently effective to process network traffic at up to 10 Gbps network speed. In order to detect known malicious traffic, Snort uses rules. If malicious traffic matches the collection of rules, alarms will be activated. But no action can be taken against unknown malicious traffic, since Snort does not use the techniques of machine learning and thus cannot block unknown malicious traffic. IDSs can be supported by machine learning techniques by accurately detecting malicious traffic and thereby reducing false positive alerts by proactively responding to unknown malicious traffic. For IDSs, such as Support Vector Machines, Decision Trees, Fuzzy Logic, BayesNet and NaiveBayes, these are different machine learning algorithms that can be used.
